  "text": "We are given a **triangular prism** with the following properties:\n\n- The **sides** form a **square**.\n- The **bases** are **equilateral triangles**.\n- The **side lengths** of the prism are $ a \\leq b \\leq c $.\n- There is a **length $ d $** which is the **altitude of the prism**.\n- The condition is: **No tetrahedron with positive volume** has side lengths $ a, b, c, d $.\n- We are to find the **maximum possible value of** $ \\frac{c}{a} $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Prism\n\nWe are told that the **sides** form a **square**. This is a key geometric constraint.\n\nLet’s think about the **structure of the prism**:\n\n- A triangular prism has two triangular bases and three rectangular lateral faces.\n- In a **right prism**, the lateral edges are perpendicular to the bases.\n- If the **sides** form a square, this implies that **three of the lateral faces are squares**.\n\nThis is only possible if **all three lateral edges are equal** and **the sides of the triangular bases are equal**.\n\nSo, the **triangular bases are equilateral triangles**, and the **lateral edges are equal in length**.\n\nLet’s define the prism:\n\n- Let the **equilateral triangle base** have side length $ a $.\n- The **height of the prism** (i.e., the length of the lateral edges) is $ b $.\n- Then the **altitude of the prism** (i.e., the height of the triangle) is $ c $.\n\nBut wait — this is confusing.\n\nLet’s clarify:\n\nLet’s define the prism in terms of:\n\n- The **equilateral triangle base** has side length $ a $.\n- The **lateral edges** (i.e., the height of the prism) is $ b $.\n- The **altitude of the prism** (i.e., the height of the triangle) is $ c $.\n\nBut that’s not right.\n\nActually, the **altitude of the prism** is the **height of the prism**, i.e., the distance between the two triangular bases.\n\nSo, if the **bases are equilateral triangles** with side length $ a $, and the **prism is right**, then the **height of the prism** is $ b $.\n\nThen, the **altitude of the triangle** is $ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a $.\n\nSo, the **altitude of the prism** is $ b $, and the **altitude of the triangle** is $ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a $.\n\nBut the problem says:\n\n> \"the side lengths of the prism are $ a, b, c $, where $ a \\leq b \\leq c $, and no tetrahedron with positive volume has side lengths $ a, b, c, d $, where $ d $ is the length of the altitude of the prism.\"\n\nSo, the **prism has side lengths** $ a, b, c $, and the **altitude of the prism** is $ d $.\n\nLet’s now try to interpret the triangle and the prism.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Understanding the Geometry\n\nLet’s define the **triangular prism**:\n\n- The **bases** are equilateral triangles with side length $ a $.\n- The **height of the prism** (i.e., the length of the lateral edges) is $ d $.\n- The **altitude of the prism** is $ d $.\n- The **altitude of the triangular base** is $ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a $.\n\nBut the problem says that the **side lengths of the prism are** $ a, b, c $, with $ a \\leq b \\leq c $.\n\nSo, we are to find the **maximum value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $**, given that **no tetrahedron with positive volume** can have side lengths $ a, b, c, d $, where $ d $ is the **altitude of the prism**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Understanding the Tetrahedron Condition\n\nThe key condition is:\n\n> No tetrahedron with **positive volume** has side lengths $ a, b, c, d $.\n\nThis means that the four lengths $ a, b, c, d $ **cannot form a tetrahedron** with positive volume.\n\nSo we are to find the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ such that **no tetrahedron** can be formed with these four lengths.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: When Can Four Lengths Form a Tetrahedron?\n\nA set of four lengths can form a **tetrahedron** if and only if they satisfy the **tetrahedron inequality** (a generalization of the triangle inequality for 3D).\n\nHowever, a simpler condition is that the **four lengths must satisfy the triangle inequality in all combinations**.\n\nIn other words, **any three of the four lengths must satisfy the triangle inequality**.\n\nSo, to **not** be able to form a tetrahedron, the four lengths must **fail the triangle inequality for at least one combination** of three lengths.\n\nSo, we need that **at least one of the following is true**:\n\n- $ a + b \\leq c $\n- $ a + c \\leq b $\n- $ b + c \\leq a $\n\nBut since $ a \\leq b \\leq c $, the only possible triangle inequality that can be violated is:\n\n$$\na + b \\leq c\n$$\n\nBecause:\n\n- $ a + c \\geq b $ is always true.\n- $ b + c \\geq a $ is always true.\n\nSo, the only possible way to **not** form a tetrahedron is if:\n\n$$\na + b \\leq c\n$$\n\nSo, we want the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ such that $ a + b \\leq c $, and the four lengths $ a, b, c, d $ **cannot form a tetrahedron**.\n\nBut we need to relate $ d $ to the prism.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Relating $ d $ to the Prism\n\nLet’s assume the prism has:\n\n- **Equilateral triangle base** with side length $ a $.\n- **Height of the prism** is $ d $.\n- The **altitude of the triangle** is $ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a $.\n\nBut we are told that the **side lengths of the prism are $ a, b, c $**, with $ a \\leq b \\leq c $, and $ d $ is the **altitude of the prism**.\n\nSo, the **three side lengths** of the prism are:\n\n- One side of the triangle base: $ a $\n- The other side of the triangle base: $ a $\n- The height of the prism: $ d $\n\nSo the prism has side lengths $ a, a, d $, but we are told the side lengths are $ a, b, c $, with $ a \\leq b \\leq c $.\n\nSo, the **three side lengths** are $ a, b, c $, and the **altitude of the prism** is $ d $.\n\nWe are to find the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ such that **no tetrahedron** can be formed with side lengths $ a, b, c, d $.\n\nSo, we must have that:\n\n$$\na + b \\leq c\n$$\n\nThis is the **only** condition that prevents the four lengths from forming a tetrahedron.\n\nSo we need to **maximize** $ \\frac{c}{a} $, given that $ a + b \\leq c $.\n\nSo we want to **maximize** $ \\frac{c}{a} $, given that $ a + b \\leq c $.\n\nLet’s define $ x = \\frac{b}{a} $, and $ y = \\frac{c}{a} $.\n\nThen $ x \\geq 1 $, $ y \\geq x $, and the condition is:\n\n$$\na + b \\leq c \\Rightarrow 1 + x \\leq y\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\ny \\geq x + 1\n$$\n\nWe want to **maximize** $ y $, given that $ y \\geq x + 1 $, and $ x \\geq 1 $.\n\nBut we also know that the **side lengths of the prism** are $ a, b, c $, and the **altitude of the prism** is $ d $.\n\nSo the prism must be a **valid prism**, and the **altitude** $ d $ must be related to the geometry of the prism.\n\nBut the problem says that **no tetrahedron** can be formed with side lengths $ a, b, c, d $.\n\nSo we must have that **the four lengths $ a, b, c, d $** cannot form a tetrahedron.\n\nBut we already assumed that $ a + b \\leq c $, which prevents the tetrahedron from being formed.\n\nSo the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ is when $ c = a + b $, and $ b $ is as large as possible.\n\nSo we want to **maximize** $ \\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a + b}{a} = 1 + \\frac{b}{a} $, so we want to **maximize** $ \\frac{b}{a} $, given that $ a + b \\leq c $, and that the prism is valid.\n\nBut since $ a \\leq b \\leq c $, and $ c = a + b $, we can now define the **maximum possible value** of $ \\frac{c}{a} $.\n\nLet’s now consider the **prism**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Final Step — Use the Prism Geometry\n\nWe are told that the **sides of the prism form a square**.\n\nThat is, the **three rectangular faces** of the prism are **squares**.\n\nSo, the **three rectangular faces** have sides $ a, d $, $ a, d $, and $ b, d $.\n\nSince the **faces are squares**, then:\n\n- $ a = d $\n- $ b = d $\n\nBut this contradicts the assumption that $ a \\leq b \\leq c $, and $ a \\leq b $.\n\nBut we are told that the **sides form a square**, and the **bases are equilateral triangles**.\n\nSo, the three rectangular faces are squares, which implies that:\n\n- Two of the sides of the prism are equal to the **height** of the prism.\n- The third side of the prism is equal to the **side of the triangle base**.\n\nSo, the **three side lengths** of the prism are:\n\n- Two sides of the base: $ a $\n- One side of the prism (height): $ d $\n\nSo the side lengths of the prism are $ a, a, d $, and the **altitude of the prism** is $ d $.\n\nSo the **three side lengths** of the prism are $ a, a, d $, and the **altitude** is $ d $.\n\nSo the **four lengths** that cannot form a tetrahedron are $ a, a, d, d $.\n\nBut the problem says the side lengths of the prism are $ a, b, c $, and the **altitude is $ d $**.\n\nSo the **four lengths** are $ a, b, c, d $, and the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ is such that **no tetrahedron** can be formed with these four lengths.\n\nSo we are to **maximize** $ \\frac{c}{a} $, given that $ a + b \\leq c $, and that the **prism has side lengths $ a, b, c $** and **altitude $ d $**.\n\nSo we can now assume:\n\n- $ a + b \\leq c $\n- $ a \\leq b \\leq c $\n- We want to **maximize** $ \\frac{c}{a} $\n\nLet’s assume $ a = 1 $, then $ b \\geq 1 $, and $ c \\geq b $.\n\nSo $ c \\geq b \\geq 1 $, and $ c \\geq 1 + b $.\n\nSo the **maximum** $ \\frac{c}{a} $ is when $ c = 1 + b $, and $ b $ is as large as possible.\n\nBut we also need the **prism to be valid**.\n\nIf the prism has side lengths $ a, b, c $, and the **altitude is $ d $**, then the **three rectangular faces** are:\n\n- $ a \\times d $\n- $ a \\times d $\n- $ b \\times d $\n\nIf all three faces are **squares**, then:\n\n- $ a = d $\n- $ b = d $\n\nBut this would mean $ a = b = d $, and $ c \\geq a + b = 2a $, so $ \\frac{c}{a} \\geq 2 $.\n\nSo the **maximum** value of $ \\frac{c}{a} $ is **2**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$",
